  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe are minimizing the evaluation function f(seq) = 2*n*max(convolve(seq, mode='full')) / (sum(seq))^2.\nOur approach combines three phases: (1) Multistart with various initialization methods, (2) Stochastic search using Gaussian mutation and crossover, and (3) Local improvement with a smoothed Adam optimizer. The core idea is to use multiple representations of sequences (greedy, power decay, uniform, dirichlet) to escape local minima. We schedule these phases based on sequence length and maintain a best-sequence dictionary across all lengths. The optimization loop explicitly tracks the global minimum and uses FFT-based evaluation for key decisions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Input: Sequence of nonnegative floats\n- Normalization: via evaluate_sequence (enforces positive sum and bounds)\n- Objective: 2*n*max( |convolve(a)| ) / (sum(a))^2\n- We consider sequences of length n where n is a power of two up to 4096\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nWe start with base_lengths = [128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]\ndef main_search(seed=42, budget_s=1800):\n    from numpy import rfft, irfft, clip\n\n    base_lengths = [128, 256, 384, 4096]\n    best_by_n = {}\n\n    for n in base_lengths:\n        # Greedy initialization\n        init1 = greedy_normalized(n)\n        score1 = evaluate_sequence(init1)\n        if score1 >= 0.001:  # Normalizes to at least 1e-3\n            best_sequence = init1\n            best_score = score1\n        else:\n            print(\"Greedy pass failed for length\", n)\n\n        # Power-based initialization\n        init2 = power_normalized(n, 0.55)\n        score2 = evaluate_sequence(init2)\n        if score2 >= 0.001 and score2 < best_score:\n            best_sequence = init2\n            best_score = score2\n\n        # Structural mixed initialization\n        init3 = mix_greedy_uniform(n, 0.75)\n        score3 = evaluate_sequence(init3)\n        if score3 >= 0.001 and score3 < best_score:\n            best_sequence = init3\n            best_score = score3\n\n        # Bayesian optimization start for higher n\n        if n >= 512 and time_left >= 60:\n            x = np.log(np.array([0.1]*n))  # Base parametric form\n            # Apply smoothing with Gaussian process kernels\n            # Calculate allocation based on remaining score\n        else:\n            # Classical initialization\n            pass\n\n    while time_left > 60 and best_score > 1.5027:\n        # Stochastic phase: use crossover and mutation from current best\n        candidate = crossover(best_sequence, n=10)  # Limited cross for sample space reduction\n        candidate = GaussianMutation(candidate, 1.0)\n        new_score = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n        if new_score >= 0.001 and new_score < best_score:\n            best_sequence = candidate\n            best_score = new_score\n\n    # Local improvement phase if n=2048 or 4096\n    if n>=1024 and budget_s>1800:\n        # Use adaptive Adam optimizer with dynamic learning rate\n        # Evaluate gradient and make local adjustments\n        pass\n\n    return best_sequence.tolist()\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\nWe prioritize:\n- Greedy_normalized for medium lengths (128-1024)\n- Power_normalized with decay factor 0.55\n- Mix_greedy_uniform for longer sequences (with interpolation parameter 0.75)\n\nParameters:\n- budget_s automatic down-sampling\n- time asleep at convergence points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nWe use FFT_convolution with time_per_evaluation = (n log n)\nTotal_ops = sum_{n} budget_fraction * n^2\nwith lazy termination at same or better score than current best\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nevalueation_sequence will be called multiple times during search.\nFinal candidate is validated against the evaluator.\nAll intermediate sequences must comply with the normalization: sum >= 0.01\nWe check for NaN and Inf in all evaluations.\n\nHelper functions:\n- greedy_normalized(n): returns sequence with alternating peaks, normalized sum 1.0\n- power_normalized(n, alpha): returns sequence decaying as (index)^(-alpha)\n- mix_greedy_uniform(n, lam): returns convex combination with uniform\n\nCritical implementation: check dimension n log n scaling during optimization.\nNote: evaluate_sequence already handles normalization.",
